 of the discriminant. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have exactly one solution, the discriminant must be zero. This is because a discriminant of zero indicates that the quadratic equation has a double root, which means it touches the x-axis at exactly one point.\n\nGiven the quadratic equation \\( 3x^2 + 4x + c = 0 \\), we identify the coefficients as:\n- \\( a = 3 \\)\n- \\( b = 4 \\)\n- \\( c = c \\)\n\nSubstitute these values into the discriminant formula:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 4^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot c\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 16 - 12c\n\\]\n\nFor the equation to have exactly one solution, set the discriminant equal to zero:\n\n\\[\n16 - 12c = 0\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\n16 = 12c\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{16}{12}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( c \\) that gives exactly one solution for \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{3}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{4}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:439"}
